        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(gateway): pre-handshake WS verifyClient with Sec-Fetch-Site and forwarded-header hardening",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's gateway validates browser `Origin` on the WebSocket path only **post-handshake** (in `src/gateway/server/ws-connection/connect-admission.ts`, after the socket is already open). That means a browser from a disallowed origin completes the HTTP 101 upgrade before the origin check runs — the socket is open, and only then is the request rejected.\n\nThis PR adds a **pre-handshake** `verifyClient` gate on the gateway `WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })` that runs the **existing** `checkBrowserOrigin` (the same origin-policy contract used post-handshake) *before* the 101. A browser from a disallowed origin never completes the upgrade.\n\nThis is intentionally narrow: **one control, no new configuration keys, no second policy surface.** It reuses `gateway.controlUi.allowedOrigins` and `gateway.controlUi.dangerouslyAllowHostHeaderOriginFallback` — the origin policy already shipped — so pre-handshake and post-handshake admission share one source of truth and one owner boundary.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nPer the review guidance to \"narrow to one proven control\" and align with the existing origin-policy direction rather than add a permanent `gateway.security.*` config surface: this PR ships only the pre-handshake origin gate. It introduces no new settings, no forwarded-header validation engine, and no `Sec-Fetch-Site` toggle — those are deliberately out of scope to avoid a second admission boundary and operator footguns.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- **Non-browser clients** (CLI, native apps) send no `Origin` and pass through; they authenticate post-handshake as upstream does today.\n- **Browser clients** are checked against `checkBrowserOrigin` before the upgrade:\n  - allowed `Origin` (allowlist / loopback / host-header fallback) → `101 Switching Protocols`.\n  - disallowed `Origin` → `403 Forbidden`, before the socket opens.\n  - a literal `null` opaque Origin is checked, not skipped — it cannot bypass the gate.\n\n## Evidence\n\nA real `node:http` server with a real `ws.WebSocketServer({ noServer: true, verifyClient })`, wired through the production `createGatewayVerifyClient`, was sent real HTTP Upgrade requests over a raw socket (the same wire path the production gateway uses — not a mock).\n\n```\n== LIVE WS UPGRADE PROOF (head 6d725bb622f) ==\n[native client, no Origin]                         -> 101 Switching Protocols  (ACCEPTED ✓)\n[allowed browser Origin (allowlist)]               -> 101 Switching Protocols  (ACCEPTED ✓)\n[loopback browser Origin localhost -> 127.0.0.1]   -> 101 Switching Protocols  (ACCEPTED ✓)\n[disallowed browser Origin]                        -> 403 Forbidden           (REJECTED ✓)\n[non-loopback browser Origin, no allowlist]        -> 403 Forbidden           (REJECTED ✓)\n[literal null opaque Origin]                       -> 403 Forbidden           (REJECTED ✓)\nRESULT: ALL CASES PASSED\n```\n\nRedacted: no tokens, endpoints, or secrets; IPs are `127.0.0.1` / `203.0.113.x` documentation ranges.\n\nUnit tests: `src/gateway/server/verify-client.test.ts` (6/6 passing).\n\n## Scope\n\n- **In scope:** `src/gateway/server/verify-client.ts` (new, wired at `server-runtime-state.ts`), its test, reusing `checkBrowserOrigin` and `gateway.controlUi.*` config that already exists.\n- **Out of scope:** forwarded-header validation, proto-mismatch, untrusted-proxy rejection, `Sec-Fetch-Site` gating, and any new config keys. Those are separate policy decisions and intentionally not bundled here.\n\n## Related\n\n- Sibling origin-policy work: #98924 (`allowedOriginPatterns`).\n",
